    "text": "# Appointment Reminder Service\n\n## Scope\n\nA small service that, given an appointment (patient, provider, start time, channel), delivers a reminder at a configured lead time (e.g., 24h and 1h before). Assume SMS/email via a provider API. Assume a Postgres database and a pool of workers.\n\n## Data model\n\n```\nappointments(id, patient_id, start_at_utc, tz, status, version)\nreminders(id, appointment_id, channel, fire_at_utc, offset_minutes,\n          status, attempt, next_attempt_at, lease_until,\n          provider_msg_id, sent_at, last_error,\n          UNIQUE(appointment_id, channel, offset_minutes))\n```\n\nKey points:\n- `start_at_utc` is stored in UTC; `tz` is the IANA zone of the appointment (needed for \"9am local\" semantics and for DST correctness).\n- `fire_at_utc` is computed once at scheduling time from `start_at_utc - offset`, using the appointment's tz. Storing it as an absolute instant avoids re-deriving it on every tick and avoids DST ambiguity at send time.\n- The unique constraint on `(appointment_id, channel, offset_minutes)` is the primary defense against duplicate reminders.\n\n## Scheduling\n\nA scheduler (cron or a loop) inserts `reminders` rows for upcoming appointments. It's idempotent: `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`. If the scheduler runs twice, or two schedulers race, no duplicates.\n\n## Delivery loop\n\nWorkers claim due reminders with a lease:\n\n```sql\nUPDATE reminders\nSET status='sending', lease_until = now() + interval '2 minutes',\n    attempt = attempt + 1\nWHERE id IN (\n  SELECT id FROM reminders",
